One common vulnerability in Java web applications is **Insecure Deserialization**, often caused by using the native `java.io.ObjectInputStream` on untrusted user input. An attacker can craft a malicious serialized object that, when deserialized, executes arbitrary commands on the server (Remote Code Execution).

```java
import java.io.*;
import javax.servlet.http.*;

// This class demonstrates an insecure way of handling serialized data
public class InsecureDeserializationHandler extends HttpServlet {
    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws IOException {
        try {
            // The application reads a serialized object directly from the HTTP request body
            // This is vulnerable to Remote Code Execution (RCE) attacks via gadget chains
            InputStream is = request.getInputStream();
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(is);

            // Deserializing untrusted data without validation allows an attacker 
            // to instantiate malicious classes and execute code.
            Object obj = ois.readObject(); 
            response.getWriter().println("Object received: " + obj.toString());
        } catch (Exception e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
